                "content": "# Membrane Topology Analysis\n\n## 1. Topology of the WT Protein (After Signal-Peptide Cleavage)\n\n| Region | Location |\n|--------|----------|\n| Residues 26\u2013179 | **Lumenal** |\n| H1 (180\u2013202) | **Transmembrane** |\n| Residues 203\u2013299 | **Cytosolic** |\n| H2 (300\u2013322) | **Transmembrane** |\n| Residues 323\u2013420 | **Lumenal** |\n\n**Evidence summary:**\n\n- **Residues 26\u2013179 (lumenal):** Residue 90 is glycosylated (lumen-only event) and is protected from protease on intact microsomes.\n- **Residues 203\u2013299 (cytosolic):** Residue 250 is *not* glycosylated, and protease digests \"most of the segment between H1 and H2\" on intact microsomes.\n- **Residues 323\u2013420 (lumenal):** Residue 350 is glycosylated and is protected from protease.\n- **H1 and H2 (transmembrane):** These are the hydrophobic segments that define the boundaries of the protected and unprotected regions; they anchor the protein in the bilayer and are inaccessible to protease.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Assignment of Stop-Transfer vs. Start-Transfer\n\n- **H1 = stop-transfer sequence**\n- **H2 = start-transfer sequence (reinitiation of translocation)**\n\n**How the glycosylation data supports this:**\n\nThe N-terminal signal peptide (residues 1\u201325) initiates translocation and is cleaved, so the nascent chain begins emerging into the lumen. As synthesis continues:\n\n1. **Residue 90** (N-terminal to H1) enters the lumen \u2192 **glycosylated** \u2713\n2. **H1 inserts and stops translocation.** The ribosome is now on the cytosolic face.\n3. **Residue 250** (between H1 and H2) is synthesized on the **cytosolic** side \u2192 **not glycosylated** \u2713\n4. **H2 reinitiates translocation.** The nascent chain re-enters the lumen.\n5. **Residue 350** (C-terminal to H2) enters the lumen \u2192 **glycosylated** \u2713\n\nIf the assignments were reversed (H1 = start, H2 = stop), residue 250 would be lumenal (and should be glycosylated) and residue 350 would be cytosolic (and should not be glycosylated) \u2014 the exact opposite of what is observed. The glycosylation pattern therefore uniquely demands H1 as stop and H2 as reinitiating start.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Why Residue 250 Is Not Glycosylated\n\nAlthough the protein enters the ER via the N-terminal signal peptide, translocation is **co-translational**. By the time the ribosome has synthesized through H1, the stop-transfer event has halted translocation and positioned the ribosome on the **cytosolic face** of the membrane. Residues 203\u2013299 (including the sequon at 250) are then synthesized entirely on the cytosolic side. They never pass through the translocon into the lumen, so the oligosaccharyltransferase \u2014 which resides in the lumenal face of the ER membrane \u2014 never encounters the sequon. The intact consensus sequence is therefore irrelevant: **glycosylation requires lumenal exposure, not merely the presence of a sequon.**\n\n---\n\n## 4. Predictions for the H2-Deletion Construct\n\n| Feature | Prediction |\n|---------|-----------|\n| C-terminal location | **Cytosolic** |\n| Residue 350 glycosylation | **Not glycosylated** |\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nDeletion of H2 removes the reinitiating start-transfer sequence. After H1 stops translocation, there is no mechanism to reinitiate translocation of the C-terminal portion. Consequently, all residues C-terminal to H1 (203\u2013420 in the deletion construct) are synthesized on the cytosolic face and remain there.\n\n- The C-terminus is cytosolic.\n- Residue 350 is in the cytosol \u2192 not glycosylated. \u2713 (matches experimental result)\n- Residue 90 remains N-terminal to H1, so it is still translocated into the lumen before the stop \u2192 still glycosylated. \u2713\n- **Protease protection agreement:** With H2 absent, every residue C-terminal to H1 is exposed to the cytosol and is therefore accessible to externally added protease. The observation that \"essentially all residues C-terminal to H1 are protease-sensitive\" directly confirms that this entire region is cytosolic \u2014 consistent with the loss of the reinitiation event.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Number of Transmembrane Segments in the Mature WT Protein\n\n**Two** membrane-spanning segments: H1 and H2.\n\n**Why the signal peptide is not counted:**\n\nThe N-terminal signal sequence (residues 1\u201325) is **cleaved** by signal peptidase within the lumen. A cleaved signal peptide is transiently associated with the translocon during initiation of translocation but is **not a permanent constituent of the mature protein**. Once cleaved, it dissociates from the membrane and is not part of the final topology. The mature protein (residues 26\u2013420) contains exactly two hydrophobic segments (H1 and H2) that span the bilayer, giving it a **two-pass** membrane topology with the N- and C-termini both in the lumen.",
                "reasoning_content": "Let me carefully work through this problem step by step.\n\n**Given information:**\n- 420 aa eukaryotic membrane protein\n- Signal peptide: residues 1-25 (cleavable, N-terminal)\n- H1: residues 180-202 (23 aa hydrophobic segment)\n- H2: residues 300-322 (23 aa hydrophobic segment)\n- Glycosylation sequons at residues 90, 250, and 350\n\n**WT results:**\n- Signal peptide is cleaved\n- Sequons at 90 and 350 are glycosylated\n- Sequon at 250 is NOT glycosylated\n- Protease on intact microsomes: removes most of segment between H1 and H2, but protected fragments containing residue 90 and residue 350 remain\n- Detergent + protease: complete digestion\n\n**H2 deletion construct:**\n- Residue 90 remains glycosylated\n- Residues 250 and 350 are NOT glycosylated\n- Essentially all residues C-terminal to H1 are protease-sensitive in intact microsomes\n\n**Key principles:**\n- N-linked glycosylation only occurs when the sequon is in the ER lumen (exposed to lumen)\n- Protease cannot cross the membrane, so it only digests cytosolic-exposed regions\n- A signal sequence (start-transfer) initiates translocation across the membrane\n- A stop-transfer sequence halts translocation and anchors the protein in the membrane with the C-terminal portion in the cytosol\n\n**Step-by-step reasoning for WT:**\n\n1. The N-terminal signal peptide (1-25) initiates translocation. The ribosome-mRNA complex docks at the SRP/SRP receptor/translocon. The signal sequence inserts into the translocon and initiates translocation. After cleavage of the signal peptide, the protein continues to be translocated into the ER lumen.\n\n2. Residues 26-179: These are N-terminal to H1. Since the signal peptide initiates translocation and is cleaved, these residues are translocated into the ER lumen. They should be lumenal.\n\n   - Glycosylation evidence: Residue 90 IS glycosylated. This confirms it's in the lumen.\n   - Protease evidence: Protected fragments containing residue 90 remain after protease treatment. This confirms residue 90 is not exposed to the cytosol (it's either lumenal or transmembrane).\n\n3. H1 (180-202): This is a hydrophobic segment. It could act as either a stop-transfer sequence or a second start-transfer sequence.\n\n   Let me think about this. If H1 is a stop-transfer sequence, then:\n   - Residues N-terminal to H1 (26-179) would be in the lumen\n   - H1 would be in the membrane\n   - Residues C-terminal to H1 would be in the cytosol\n   - Then H2 would need to act as a start-transfer sequence to bring the C-terminal portion back into the lumen\n\n   If H1 is a start-transfer sequence (reinitiation):\n   - Wait, but the protein is already being translocated. If H1 is a stop-transfer, the C-terminal portion goes to cytosol. Then H2 could be a start-transfer (reinitiation) that brings the C-terminal portion back into the lumen.\n\n   Let me check with the data:\n\n   **If H1 = stop-transfer, H2 = start-transfer (reinitiation):**\n   - Residues 26-179: lumenal \u2713 (residue 90 glycosylated \u2713, protected from protease \u2713)\n   - H1: transmembrane \u2713\n   - Residues 203-299: cytosolic (between the stop and the next start)\n     - Residue 250 is in this region \u2192 cytosolic \u2192 NOT glycosylated \u2713\n     - This segment should be protease-sensitive \u2713 (protease removes \"most of the segment between H1 and H2\")\n   - H2: transmembrane \u2713\n   - Residues 323-420: lumenal (C-terminal to the reinitiation)\n     - Residue 350 is in this region \u2192 lumenal \u2192 glycosylated \u2713\n     - Protected from protease \u2713\n\n   This fits ALL the data perfectly!\n\n   **If H1 = start-transfer, H2 = stop-transfer:**\n   - Residues 26-179: lumenal (from initial signal peptide translocation)\n   - H1: transmembrane\n   - Residues 203-299: lumenal (translocated after reinitiation)\n     - Residue 250 would be lumenal \u2192 should be glycosylated \u2717 (but it's NOT glycosylated)\n   - H2: transmembrane (stop)\n   - Residues 323-420: cytosolic\n     - Residue 350 would be cytosolic \u2192 NOT glycosylated \u2717 (but it IS glycosylated)\n\n   This contradicts the data. So H1 is the stop-transfer and H2 is the start-transfer (reinitiation).\n\n4. **H2 deletion construct:**\n   - Without H2, the start-transfer (reinitiation) is missing\n   - So after H1 (stop-transfer), the C-terminal portion (residues 203-420) remains in the cytosol\n   - Residue 90 is still N-terminal to H1, so still lumenal \u2192 still glycosylated \u2713\n   - Residue 250 is C-terminal to H1 \u2192 cytosolic \u2192 NOT glycosylated \u2713\n   - Residue 350 is C-terminal to H1 \u2192 cytosolic \u2192 NOT glycosylated \u2713\n   - All residues C-terminal to H1 are in the cytosol \u2192 protease-sensitive \u2713\n\n   This all fits perfectly.\n\n**Now let me answer each question:**\n\n**Question 1: Topology of WT after signal peptide cleavage**\n- Residues 26-179: LUMENAL\n- H1 (180-202): TRANSMEMBRANE\n- Residues 203-299: CYTOSOLIC\n- H2 (300-322): TRANSMEMBRANE\n- Residues 323-420: LUMENAL\n\n**Question 2: Which is stop-transfer and which is start-transfer?**\n- H1 = stop-transfer sequence\n- H2 = start-transfer sequence (reinitiation)\n\nGlycosylation support:\n- Residue 90 (N-terminal to H1) is glycosylated \u2192 lumenal \u2192 consistent with H1 being a stop (everything N-terminal to stop is lumenal)\n- Residue 250 (between H1 and H2) is NOT glycosylated \u2192 cytosolic \u2192 consistent with H1 being a stop (C-terminal to stop is cytosolic) and H2 being a start (which would bring C-terminal portion back to lumen, but residue 250 is before H2)\n- Residue 350 (C-terminal to H2) is glycosylated \u2192 lumenal \u2192 consistent with H2 being a start-transfer (reinitiation brings C-terminal portion into lumen)\n\n**Question 3: Why is residue 250 not glycosylated?**\nResidue 250 is located in the cytosolic loop between H1 (stop-transfer) and H2 (start-transfer/reinitiation). Since glycosylation only occurs in the ER lumen, and this region is exposed to the cytosol, the sequon is never presented to the oligosaccharyltransferase in the lumen. The protein does enter the ER (via the N-terminal signal peptide), but this particular region is transiently (or permanently) on the cytosolic side and never passes through the lumen.\n\nWait, I need to be more careful here. During translocation, the protein is being synthesized co-translationally. Let me think about the timing:\n\n- Signal peptide (1-25) initiates translocation\n- Residues 26-179 are translocated into the lumen as they are synthesized\n- H1 (180-202) acts as a stop-transfer: it inserts into the membrane and halts translocation\n- Residues 203-299 are synthesized on the cytosolic side of the membrane (they never enter the lumen)\n- H2 (300-322) acts as a start-transfer (reinitiation): it reinitiates translocation\n- Residues 323-420 are translocated into the lumen\n\nSo residue 250 is synthesized while the ribosome is on the cytosolic face of the membrane (after stop-transfer at H1 and before reinitiation at H2). It is never in the lumen, so it cannot be glycosylated.\n\n**Question 4: H2 deletion**\n- Without H2, there is no reinitiation of translocation\n- After H1 stops translocation, all C-terminal residues (203-420) remain on the cytosolic side\n- C-terminus: CYTOSOLIC\n- Residue 350: NOT glycosylated (it's in the cytosol)\n- This agrees with protease protection: all residues C-terminal to H1 are exposed to the cytosol and thus accessible to externally added protease \u2192 they are digested\n\n**Question 5: Number of transmembrane segments in mature WT protein**\n- H1 and H2 are the two transmembrane segments\n- Total: 2 transmembrane segments\n- The signal peptide (1-25) is NOT counted because:\n  1. It is cleaved (it's not part of the mature protein)\n  2. Even if we considered it, signal peptides that are cleaved transiently insert into the membrane during translocation but are removed from the membrane after cleavage. They are not permanent membrane-spanning segments of the mature protein.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The signal peptide is cleaved by signal peptidase in the lumen. Once cleaved, it's no longer associated with the membrane. The mature protein (residues 26-420) has exactly 2 transmembrane segments (H1 and H2).\n\nLet me make sure my answer is complete and well-organized.\n"
